  "Compute and Energy Costs Impose Hard Ceilings on AI Scaling" — A Belief within skeptic discourse that identifies the financial limits at which AI development and deployment become economically unfeasible due to the high costs of computing power and energy. 
Encompasses: The specific financial thresholds where the expense of processing power and electricity makes AI projects unsustainable or impractical to scale.
  Vulnerability: The claim of "hard ceilings" may be premature: the history of computing is replete with instances where apparent resource constraints were dissolved by architectural innovation (e.g., GPU parallelism, sparse models, distillation, mixture-of-experts). If AI-specific hardware (e.g., neuromorphic chips, photonic computing) or dramatically cheaper energy (e.g., fusion, distributed solar) arrives, current cost projections become obsolete, making this a soft constraint rather than a hard ceiling.

